      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1283,
      "output": "**Water** is a chemical substance that is essential for life on Earth. Chemically, it is defined by the formula **H₂O**, which means it is composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, how it is structured, and why it is unique:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\n*   **The Molecule:** Water is a molecule formed when two hydrogen atoms bond to a single oxygen atom via covalent bonds.\n*   **Polarity:** Water is a **polar molecule**. This means the oxygen atom pulls the shared electrons closer to itself, giving the oxygen a slight negative charge and the hydrogens a slight positive charge. This creates a \"magnetic\" effect that allows water molecules to stick together (hydrogen bonding) and to interact with other charged substances.\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is unique because it can exist naturally in all three states of matter under standard conditions on Earth:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When water is cold enough, the molecules slow down and lock into a rigid, crystalline structure.\n*   **Liquid (Water):** This is the state we encounter most often. The molecules move freely but are still attracted to one another.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water molecules gain enough energy to break free from the liquid and float into the air.\n\n### 3. Key Properties\nWater has several physical properties that make it unlike most other substances:\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it is the main transport mechanism in the human body and the environment.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules at the surface stick together tightly, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows small insects to walk on water and helps plants pull water up from their roots.\n*   **High Specific Heat:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before it gets hot, and it releases a lot of heat before it freezes. This helps regulate the Earth's climate and keeps the human body temperature stable.\n\n### 4. Occurrence\nWater is the most abundant molecule on the surface of the Earth. It covers about 71% of the planet and is found in oceans, lakes, rivers, clouds, rain, and underground aquifers. It is also found in the atmosphere and in the cells of every living organism."
